<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Rules of the Road</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for a simple task. Interactive: Create responses to class survey questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple instructions for taking transportation to a specific location (e.g., "To travel from Changuniolo to Panama City, first go to the bus terminal and purchase your ticket. Then, board the bus heading to Albrook Terminal. The trip takes about ten hours, so bring snacks and water. Once you arrive, you can take a taxi or the metro to your final destination.") Can create responses to survey questions about transportation preferences (e.g., "Answer with your preference: bus or car, and why.") Can write simple instructions about road safety rules (e.g., Some road safety rules are: wear your seatbelt in the car, do not cross the street when the red light is on, and walk on the left side of the sidewalk.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Traffic Light Sentences" activity. The teacher displays three large traffic light cards (red, yellow, green). Students are given sentence starters related to road rules and transportation. They must complete the sentence and then decide if it's a "stop" (bad idea/dangerous), "caution" (suggestion), or "go" (good idea/obligation). For example: "You ______ (wear) a seatbelt." (Green light - obligation). "Sometimes people ______ (drive) too fast." (Red light - dangerous). The focus is on activating vocabulary and grammar from previous lessons in a written context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple, clear instructions or safety rules on the board, emphasizing sequence and the use of modal verbs. For example, "To cross the street safely: First, look left. Then, look right. Next, listen for traffic. Finally, walk quickly across the sidewalk." The teacher also models how to write a short response to a survey question, e.g., "I prefer the bus because it is cheaper and more convenient." The teacher stresses multisyllabic words like "convenience" and "passenger" as they write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing long stories or short, clear instructions/answers?" (Expected answer: Short, clear instructions/answers.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of verbs are useful when giving advice or rules?" (Expected answer: Modals like 'should,' 'must,' 'have to'.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we compare things like 'bus' and 'car,' what kind of adjectives do we use?" (Expected answer: Comparatives like 'faster,' 'cheaper,' 'more convenient'.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, written "Public Transportation User Guide" (e.g., for the Panama Metro or a local bus service) and a simple "Road Safety Survey" on a handout. The user guide will include bulleted instructions using sequence words and modals (e.g., "First, you must purchase a metro card," "You should always tap in when boarding," "Do not get off the bus while it is moving"). The survey will feature questions about transportation preferences and safety habits. Both texts integrate target vocabulary (e.g., `fare`, `schedule`, `passenger`, `board`, `get on`, `get off`, `seatbelt`, `traffic light`, `sidewalk`, `traffic`, `convenient`, `efficient`, `cheapest`, `safest`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students are able to understand and produce written information and instructions related to transportation and road safety. Students recognize and use modal verbs for obligation and advice, comparatives/superlatives for preferences, and relevant phrasal verbs in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the "Public Transportation User Guide" and answer True/False questions (e.g., "You have to buy a ticket before boarding the metro. (T/F)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the "Road Safety Survey" questions and identify the modal verbs and comparative adjectives used.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: Students match vocabulary words from the texts (e.g., `fare`, `delay`, `rush hour`) to their definitions.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using modal verbs for advice and obligation, comparatives and superlatives for transportation choices, and phrasal verbs related to travel. They practice constructing clear, concise instructions and survey responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill/Cloze):</b> Students complete sentences with the correct modal verb (should, must, have to) or comparative/superlative adjective based on context. Example: "Pedestrians ______ (follow/must follow) traffic lights." "The plane is the _______ (fast) way to travel." "A bus is _______ (cheap) a taxi." They also fill in blanks with appropriate phrasal verbs: "You need to _______ (get on/off) the bus at the next stop."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Transformation):</b> Students rewrite sentences to include different grammatical structures or vocabulary. For example: "It is a good idea to wear a helmet when cycling." → "You _______ (wear) a helmet when cycling." "Trains travel very quickly, faster than cars." → "Trains are _______ (fast) than cars." Students also transform active voice to passive voice for general rules if applicable, or switch verbs for phrasal verbs (e.g. "enter the bus" → "get on the bus").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction/Peer Check - Guided Writing):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of incomplete instructions or survey questions. They collaboratively write complete sentences. For example, "How to cross the street safely:" (Students write 2-3 steps). "Why do you prefer public transportation?" (Students write 1-2 sentences with reasons). They then exchange their written work with another pair and provide feedback on clarity, grammar, and vocabulary usage, focusing on word stress in words like "transportation" and differentiating between vowel sounds like /æ/ in "passenger" and /ɑː/ in "port."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Product. Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to produce two short written pieces.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: "Local Road Safety Brochure"</b> - Each group will choose 3-4 important road safety rules specific to their local context (e.g., related to pedestrians, cyclists, or drivers). They will write these rules as clear, concise instructions using modal verbs (e.g., "You must wear a seatbelt," "Pedestrians should use the sidewalk," "Drivers have to stop at red lights"). They will also emphasize the benefits of following these rules (pragmatic function of emphasizing safety). Each rule should be 1-2 sentences long.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: "My Transportation Survey Responses"</b> - Students individually answer a short survey about their personal transportation preferences and habits, inspired by the "Road Safety Survey" from the Presentation stage. They must use comparatives/superlatives and target vocabulary to explain their choices. For example: "Which is better, bus or car, and why?" (e.g., "The bus is often more convenient and cheaper than a car, especially in rush hour.") "What is the safest way to travel?" (e.g., "I think the train is the safest for long distances.") They will aim to write 2-3 sentences for each response, ensuring proper word stress in words like "transportation" and "schedule."</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher circulates, providing support and monitoring for correct grammar and vocabulary usage, focusing on the target writing outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their group "Local Road Safety Brochure" and individual "My Transportation Survey Responses."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Formative Rubric):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Instructions and responses are clear, logical, and easy to understand. (Writing instructions for a simple task, creating responses to survey questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correct use of modal verbs for advice/obligation (e.g., `should`, `must`, `have to`) and comparatives/superlatives (e.g., `faster`, `cheapest`, `more convenient`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words related to transportation and road safety (e.g., `seatbelt`, `traffic light`, `sidewalk`, `passenger`, `commute`, `avoid`, `convenience`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy:</b> Minimal errors in spelling, punctuation, and sentence structure at A2.4 level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (for group task):</b> Evident participation from all group members in the brochure creation.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection before leaving:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new rule I can now write about road safety is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to use _______ (modals/comparatives/phrasal verbs) when writing because..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing instructions or survey responses do I need more practice with?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can remembering to use modal verbs and comparatives make my writing clearer?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., The sequential activities helped scaffold the writing task, the group work fostered collaboration and peer learning.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future writing tasks? (e.g., Provide more sentence starters for lower-proficiency students, dedicate more time to pre-writing brainstorming for complex instructions, review specific phrasal verbs related to travel more thoroughly.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: The content from this writing lesson (safety rules, transportation choices) will directly feed into Lesson 5, where students will mediate and integrate information, potentially by creating a presentation or infographic about local transportation and safety protocols as part of their 21st Century Skills Project.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>